Linux printing
==============

RISC OS on Linux prints through Linux's CUPS. In !Printers, "Linux" is a
PostScript Level 2 printer whose connection is the file
IXFS:$.riscos.print.Default; each job written there is printed by CUPS
(riscos-print, from Updates), which turns the PostScript into what the
printer takes.

The list
  The printers CUPS can print on, with their state and how they're
  connected. Network printers that need no driver (IPP Everywhere,
  AirPrint: most made since about 2012) appear by themselves when they're
  switched on, as do USB printers that speak IPP over USB ("USB"); the
  ones added here say "Added".

  Click a printer to choose it for the buttons below the list. The radio
  button beside it is the one "Linux" prints on: Make default (or click
  the radio button), then Set. With none ticked, CUPS chooses (its
  default, or the first it found).

  Options...  The printer's settings, as CUPS has them (paper size, paper
              source, quality, colour and the rest): choose from each
              menu, then Set.
  Test page   CUPS's test page on that printer.
  Remove      Takes away a printer added here (the ones found by
              themselves go when they're switched off).
  Add...      A printer that doesn't appear by itself: one Linux has found
              that needs a driver, or its address (192.168.0.50, a host
              name, or ipp://, ipps://, socket://, lpd:// ...) and, if you
              like, a name. Linux asks the printer what it can do; a
              printer that isn't IPP (socket://, lpd://) is given the
              PostScript as it is.

CUPS web page
  CUPS's own pages (http://localhost:631/) in the web browser, with every
  setting it has. Changes there ask for root's user name and password.

Status
  The last job, whether CUPS is running, and whether !Printers has
  "Linux" (Set up puts it there: quit !Printers and run it again to see
  it).

Other printers: printing to IXFS:$.riscos.print.NAME (a File connection)
prints on the one called NAME.

